# size-ratchet exclusion list — pattern<TAB>reason, glob against the full
# repo-relative path, `*` crosses `/`. See third_party/size-ratchet/README.md.
#
# Divergence from the fleet's 400/800 split, stated per the 2026-08-10 audit:
# the engine exposes ONE threshold, so the fleet's 800-line test allowance
# cannot be expressed here. Oversized Go `_test.go` files and the
# `scripts/test-*.js` harnesses simply enter the baseline instead — acceptable,
# since a baselined test still cannot grow, and it needs no second gate.
#
# THREE BASELINED SIZE EXCEPTIONS, argued and granted (VGS-155, 2026-08-16), and
# recorded here because a baseline row is `path<TAB>digits` and cannot carry a
# comment — the engine rejects both a third field and a `#` line as malformed:
#
#   scripts/lib/vendor-drift.sh            429 lines, 254 of them code
#   scripts/test-vendor-drift-evidence.sh  420 lines, 279 of them code
#   scripts/test-vendor-drift.sh           434 lines, 269 of them code
#
# vendor-drift.sh's row was RAISED from 422 to 429, with sign-off, which is a
# stronger act than granting a new row: tighten-only exists precisely to forbid
# growth of a baselined file. It bought a guard refusing a DOUBLE-quoted `Only
# in` component. Without it the at-risk list printed `m/"'doomed.txt'"` — a file
# that does not exist — as the target of an `rsync --delete`. The three shapes
# deferred to VGS-178 record the raw diff line instead, which reads as "could
# not attribute"; this one made a confident wrong statement about what was about
# to be deleted, which is the sentence VGS-155 was filed to make impossible.
#
# All three are MAJORITY CODE, and the growth is rounds of reviewer-demanded
# fixes rather than padding. The prose-to-guard audit already ran on the library
# and converted everything convertible — 425 to 369 with code unchanged — which
# REMOVED an earlier exception rather than arguing for one; what remains is the
# residue that cannot execute. None is excluded: they stay baselined, so the
# ratchet still holds them tighten-only and the number can only ever go DOWN.
#
# The third was granted last and separately. test-vendor-drift.sh sat at 399,
# one line under the threshold, until a reviewer found a repair branch no case
# drove — the overridden merged deletion. Pinning it is a case, and a case is
# how a suite is supposed to grow. The alternatives were a split (a new file, a
# manifest row and a CI change, to avoid a 22-line overage) or shipping the fix
# with nothing driving its branch, which is the false confidence this whole
# issue is about.
#
# THE RECURRENCE IS THE FINDING, and it is recorded here rather than left as
# four independent good calls. VGS-155 took FOUR size accommodations: two rows
# granted originally, a third for test-vendor-drift.sh, and then vendor-drift.sh
# raised 422 → 429. Each was argued on its own merits and each is defensible.
# The sequence is a signal none of them saw: a single 400-line cap is repeatedly
# obstructing correctness work on one small cluster of files, and every reviewer
# finding on them arrives as a size argument before it can be fixed.
#
# Two mechanisms drive it. A suite grows by exactly the process's own mechanism —
# one more case per finding — so a cap on a suite taxes coverage. And a library
# whose comments ARE the evidence (the incident, the shapes that defeated two
# prefix tests) cannot shed lines without shedding the reason its guards exist.
# The fleet's 800-line test allowance would have covered the first; this engine
# exposes one threshold, so it is not expressible.
#
# That is a deliberate decision to take, not a fifth argument to win.
#
# A FOURTH EXCEPTION, granted on the same terms (VGS-150, 2026-08-16), recorded
# here so the paragraph above can be read against one more data point rather
# than three:
#
#   scripts/check-section-pointers.py      478 lines, 377 of them code
#
# The `<doc>.md § <section>` pointer guard. It is majority code, and the growth
# is five review rounds — 23 findings, then 12, then 6 — each of which asked for
# another rule, another arm, or another reason written down. Its subsystem is
# ten files and 2,827 lines, and every seam anyone has named is already taken:
# three parser libraries (prose_blocks for what counts as prose, pointer_targets
# for which document a token names, section_pointers for where a name ends and
# whether a heading matches), one VCS-access library (tracked_blobs), and five
# control scripts — one per subject, each beside what it pins.
#
# What is left in this file is one concept that does not divide: a check's own
# policy. Its five exclusion tables, the four collection points that assert them,
# and the run that turns those into a verdict. Splitting policy from the check
# that applies it would put the tables a reader needs in a second file, and the
# only remaining candidate — the scope layer — was offered and not taken for
# that reason.
#
# It also demonstrates the SECOND mechanism the paragraph above names, from the
# other side. Each new control script costs two lines in scripts/validate and one
# in ci.yml, and this round's split pushed those two files over their own rows.
# Past a certain size, splitting to satisfy the cap grows the files that wire the
# splits together. That is the same single-threshold problem, met by a check
# rather than a suite.
backend/vendor/*	vendored Go modules (go mod vendor for offline Nix builds), upstream-owned
config/vshell/nvim/colorschemes/*	vendored upstream colorscheme plugins, upstream-owned
third_party/*	byte-pinned vendored trees (review-gate, size-ratchet, coderabbit-schema, asdcontrol)
quickshell/*/assets/*	shipped binary/data assets, not source
themes/*/apps/*	generated per-app theme outputs inside theme packages, not source
themes/*/backgrounds/*	shipped background images inside theme packages, not source
themes/*/theme.json	theme-package palette data, not source
themes/*/colors.toml	theme-package palette data, not source
themes/*/preview.png	theme-package preview image, not source
themes/catalog.json	generated theme catalog index, regenerated from packages
